<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <meta name="description"
    content="Fatal Roads: An interactive visual data story exploring where, when, and under what conditions fatal traffic crashes occurred in the United States in 2024.">

  <title>Fatal Roads | The Story Behind America's Fatal Crashes</title>

  <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/style.css">

  <!-- Storytelling enhancements.
       Move these styles to css/style.css later. -->
  <style>
    html {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
      scroll-padding-top: 90px;
    }

    .story-progress {
      position: fixed;
      top: 0;
      left: 0;
      width: 100%;
      height: 4px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.08);
      z-index: 1000;
      pointer-events: none;
    }

    #story-progress-fill {
      width: 0;
      height: 100%;
      background: #e97967;
      transition: width 0.12s linear;
    }

    .story-chapter {
      scroll-margin-top: 100px;
    }

    .story-bridge,
    .story-conclusion {
      max-width: 900px;
      margin: 64px auto;
      padding: 30px 24px;
      border-left: 3px solid #e97967;
    }

    .story-bridge p,
    .story-conclusion p {
      line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .story-insight {
      margin-top: 20px;
      padding: 20px 24px;
      background: rgba(233,121,103,0.07);
      border-left: 3px solid #e97967;
      border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }

    .story-insight p,
    .story-method-note,
    .story-caveat {
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      opacity: 0.85;
    }

    .story-next {
      margin: 28px 0 12px;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .story-next a,
    .story-text-link {
      color: #f0a18f;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 4px;
    }

    .story-actions {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 20px;
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .story-button {
      display: inline-block;
      padding: 13px 22px;
      background: #c95d4d;
      color: white;
      border-radius: 7px;
      text-decoration: none;
      font-weight: 650;
    }

    .story-button:hover {
      background: #e27664;
    }

    .story-signature-card {
      min-height: 320px;
    }

    #fatality-signature {
      min-height: 220px;
    }

    #relationships {
      scroll-margin-top: 100px;
    }

    .story-section-label {
      color: #f0a18f;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .story-steps {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(
        auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr)
      );
      gap: 14px;
      margin-top: 25px;
    }

    .story-step {
      padding: 18px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.04);
      border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.09);
      border-radius: 8px;
    }

    .story-step strong {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      color: #f0a18f;
    }

    .story-step p {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    .signature-controls {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 12px;
    }

    .signature-controls label {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 10px;
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      html {
        scroll-behavior: auto;
      }

      #story-progress-fill {
        transition: none;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <!-- D3.js v7 -->
  <script
    src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/d3@7/dist/d3.min.js"
    defer>
  </script>

  <!-- TopoJSON -->
  <script
    src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/topojson-client@3/dist/topojson-client.min.js"
    defer>
  </script>

  <!-- Existing D3 application -->
  <script type="module" src="js/main.js"></script>
</head>

<body>

  <!-- ======================================
       STORY PROGRESS
       ====================================== -->

  <div class="story-progress" aria-hidden="true">
    <div id="story-progress-fill"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ======================================
       HEADER AND NAVIGATION
       ====================================== -->

  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">

      <div class="brand">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">
          FR
        </span>

        <div>
          <h1>FATAL ROADS</h1>
          <p>U.S. Traffic Fatalities | 2024</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <nav class="main-nav"
           aria-label="Story navigation">

        <a href="#overview">Start</a>
        <a href="#geography">Where</a>
        <a href="#time">When</a>
        <a href="#conditions">Conditions</a>
        <a href="#relationships">Compare</a>
        <a href="#signature">Synthesis</a>
        <a href="#explorer">Explore</a>

      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="dashboard story-layout"
        id="main-content">

    <!-- ======================================
         INTRODUCTION
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="overview"
      class="hero"
      aria-labelledby="overview-title">

      <p class="eyebrow">
        NHTSA FARS · 2024
      </p>

      <h2 id="overview-title">
        36,297 Fatal Crashes.
        One Year.
        What Patterns Emerge?
      </h2>

      <p class="hero-description">
        In 2024, fatal motor vehicle crashes
        affected communities across the
        United States.
      </p>

      <p class="hero-description">
        But a national total cannot reveal
        where these crashes occurred,
        when they happened, or the
        circumstances recorded at the scene.
      </p>

      <p class="hero-description">
        Fatal Roads follows the evidence
        through geography, time, recorded
        conditions, and state-level comparisons
        to investigate the patterns behind
        the national numbers.
      </p>

      <div class="story-actions">

        <a href="#geography"
           class="story-button">
          Begin the Story ↓
        </a>

        <a href="#explorer"
           class="story-text-link">
          Explore the Data Yourself
        </a>

      </div>

      <p class="story-caveat">
        This story examines recorded fatal
        crash counts, not individual driving
        risk or crash causation.
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         NATIONAL OVERVIEW
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      class="kpi-grid"
      aria-label="National fatal crash statistics">

      <article class="kpi-card">
        <span>Total Fatal Crashes</span>
        <strong id="total-crashes">—</strong>
        <small>
          Reported fatal crashes in 2024
        </small>
      </article>

      <article class="kpi-card">
        <span>Total Fatalities</span>
        <strong id="total-fatalities">—</strong>
        <small>
          People killed in fatal crashes
        </small>
      </article>

      <article class="kpi-card">
        <span>Geographic Coverage</span>
        <strong id="total-states">—</strong>
        <small>
          States and Washington, D.C.
        </small>
      </article>

      <article class="kpi-card">
        <span>
          Average Fatalities per Crash
        </span>
        <strong id="average-fatalities">—</strong>
        <small>
          Fatalities divided by fatal crashes
        </small>
      </article>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CENTRAL STORY QUESTION
         ====================================== -->

    <section class="story-bridge"
             aria-labelledby="question-title">

      <p class="eyebrow">
        THE CENTRAL QUESTION
      </p>

      <h2 id="question-title">
        What Do National Totals Hide?
      </h2>

      <p>
        A single number summarizes the
        scale of fatal crashes, but it
        cannot describe the differences
        across states, months, hours,
        or recorded conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        To understand these patterns,
        we need to examine the data
        from several perspectives.
      </p>

      <div class="story-steps">

        <div class="story-step">
          <strong>01 · Where?</strong>
          <p>
            Explore geographic differences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="story-step">
          <strong>02 · When?</strong>
          <p>
            Investigate temporal patterns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="story-step">
          <strong>03 · Conditions?</strong>
          <p>
            Examine recorded circumstances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="story-step">
          <strong>04 · Comparison</strong>
          <p>
            Compare state-level relationships.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="story-step">
          <strong>05 · Synthesis</strong>
          <p>
            Bring the dimensions together.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CHAPTER 1: GEOGRAPHY
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="geography"
      class="chart-section story-chapter"
      data-chapter="1"
      aria-labelledby="geography-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            CHAPTER 01 / GEOGRAPHY
          </p>

          <h2 id="geography-title">
            Where Do Fatal Crashes Occur?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fatal crashes were recorded
            across the country, but their
            totals vary by state.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first step is to examine
            how fatal crashes are distributed
            geographically and how that
            picture changes when comparing
            crashes with fatalities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <select
          id="map-metric"
          aria-label="Select state map metric">

          <option value="crashes">
            Fatal Crashes
          </option>

          <option value="fatalities">
            Fatalities
          </option>

        </select>

      </div>

      <!-- EXISTING CHOROPLETH CONTAINER -->
      <div
        class="chart-card chart-large"
        id="state-map"
        aria-label="Interactive U.S. state choropleth map">

        <p class="chart-placeholder"
           role="status">
          Loading state map...
        </p>

      </div>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          Reading the Geographic Evidence
        </strong>

        <p>
          Darker states represent higher
          recorded totals for the selected
          metric. Switch between crashes
          and fatalities to compare the
          two measurements.
        </p>

        <p>
          These values are not adjusted
          for population, traffic volume,
          or miles driven. Higher counts
          do not necessarily indicate
          higher individual risk.
        </p>

      </aside>

      <p class="story-next">
        Geography reveals where crashes
        were recorded, but location alone
        cannot explain their distribution.

        <a href="#time">
          Next: When do fatal crashes happen? →
        </a>
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CHAPTER 2: TEMPORAL PATTERNS
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="time"
      class="chart-section story-chapter"
      data-chapter="2"
      aria-labelledby="time-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            CHAPTER 02 / TEMPORAL PATTERNS
          </p>

          <h2 id="time-title">
            When Do Fatal Crashes Happen?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Geographic differences tell
            only part of the story.
            Fatal crashes also vary
            across time.
          </p>

          <p>
            We examine time at two scales:
            monthly trends across 2024
            and recurring weekday-hour
            patterns.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="chart-grid">

        <!-- EXISTING MONTHLY LINE CHART -->
        <article class="chart-card">

          <div class="chart-title-row">

            <h3 id="monthly-title">
              Monthly Crash Trends
            </h3>

            <select
              id="monthly-metric"
              aria-label="Select monthly metric">

              <option value="crashes">
                Fatal Crashes
              </option>

              <option value="fatalities">
                Fatalities
              </option>

            </select>

          </div>

          <p>
            How did recorded fatal crash
            totals change from January
            through December?
          </p>

          <div
            id="monthly-trend"
            class="chart-content"
            aria-labelledby="monthly-title">

            <p class="chart-placeholder"
               role="status">
              Loading monthly trend...
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

        <!-- EXISTING TEMPORAL HEATMAP -->
        <article class="chart-card">

          <h3 id="temporal-title">
            Weekday × Hour Distribution
          </h3>

          <p>
            Monthly totals hide the
            repeating patterns within
            each week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Explore which weekday-hour
            combinations contain more
            recorded fatal crashes.
          </p>

          <div
            id="temporal-heatmap"
            class="chart-content"
            aria-labelledby="temporal-title">

            <p class="chart-placeholder"
               role="status">
              Loading temporal heatmap...
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

      </div>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          Reading the Temporal Evidence
        </strong>

        <p>
          The line chart shows changes
          across months, while the heatmap
          shows how recorded crashes are
          distributed across weekdays
          and hours.
        </p>

        <p>
          Select data points to inspect
          specific time periods.
          A higher count indicates more
          recorded fatal crashes, not
          necessarily a higher crash rate
          per trip or mile traveled.
        </p>

      </aside>

      <p class="story-next">
        We now know how to examine
        the distribution across place
        and time.

        <a href="#conditions">
          Next: Under what conditions
          were these crashes recorded? →
        </a>
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CHAPTER 3: RECORDED CONDITIONS
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="conditions"
      class="chart-section story-chapter"
      data-chapter="3"
      aria-labelledby="conditions-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            CHAPTER 03 / CRASH CONDITIONS
          </p>

          <h2 id="conditions-title">
            What Conditions Are Recorded?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The circumstances surrounding
            a crash add another dimension
            to the story.
          </p>

          <p>
            The FARS dataset records
            weather, lighting, and
            rural or urban classification.
            Comparing these categories
            helps describe the conditions
            present during fatal crashes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <select
          id="condition-type"
          aria-label="Select crash condition category">

          <option value="weather">
            Weather
          </option>

          <option value="lighting">
            Lighting
          </option>

          <option value="ruralUrban">
            Rural / Urban
          </option>

        </select>

      </div>

      <!-- EXISTING CONDITION BAR CHART -->
      <article class="chart-card">

        <h3 id="condition-chart-title">
          Recorded Crash Conditions
        </h3>

        <p>
          Compare the number of fatal
          crashes across categories.
          Use the selector to switch
          between weather, lighting,
          and rural/urban classifications.
        </p>

        <div
          id="condition-bar"
          class="chart-content"
          aria-labelledby="condition-chart-title">

          <p class="chart-placeholder"
             role="status">
            Loading condition chart...
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          An Important Distinction
        </strong>

        <p>
          A condition appearing frequently
          in fatal crash records does not
          mean that condition caused the
          crashes.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, comparing the
          number of crashes in clear
          and rainy weather does not
          establish which condition
          has greater risk without
          knowing how much driving
          occurred in each condition.
        </p>

      </aside>

      <p class="story-next">
        Conditions describe the
        circumstances of crashes.
        Next, we examine how crash
        and fatality totals relate
        across states.

        <a href="#relationships">
          Next: Comparing states →
        </a>
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CHAPTER 4: STATE RELATIONSHIPS
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="relationships"
      class="chart-section story-chapter"
      data-chapter="4"
      aria-labelledby="relationships-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            CHAPTER 04 / STATE COMPARISONS
          </p>

          <h2 id="relationships-title">
            Do More Fatal Crashes
            Always Mean More Fatalities?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crash totals and fatality
            totals describe related,
            but different, quantities.
          </p>

          <p>
            One fatal crash may involve
            more than one death.
            Comparing both measurements
            can reveal differences in
            average fatalities per crash.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- EXISTING BUBBLE SCATTERPLOT -->
      <article class="chart-card">

        <h3 id="scatter-title">
          State Crash Relationships
        </h3>

        <p>
          Each bubble represents a state.
          Compare fatal crashes,
          recorded fatalities, and
          average fatalities per crash.
        </p>

        <div
          id="bubble-scatter"
          class="chart-content"
          aria-labelledby="scatter-title">

          <p class="chart-placeholder"
             role="status">
            Loading bubble scatterplot...
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          Looking Beyond Raw Totals
        </strong>

        <p>
          Fatal crashes and fatalities
          are closely related by definition,
          but they are not identical.
          Comparing their relationship
          helps distinguish the number
          of crashes from the number
          of people killed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Average fatalities per fatal
          crash is a descriptive measure.
          It does not establish the
          probability of a crash occurring.
        </p>

      </aside>

      <p class="story-next">
        So far, each visualization
        has examined a different
        part of the dataset.

        <a href="#signature">
          Next: Bringing the patterns
          together →
        </a>
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         CHAPTER 5: INNOVATIVE VIEW
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="signature"
      class="chart-section story-chapter"
      data-chapter="5"
      aria-labelledby="signature-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            CHAPTER 05 / SYNTHESIS
          </p>

          <h2 id="signature-title">
            Every State Has a
            Fatal-Crash Signature
          </h2>

          <p>
            The previous chapters examined
            geography, time, conditions,
            and fatalities separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            But what happens when we
            combine these dimensions
            into a single visual
            representation?
          </p>

          <p>
            The Fatality Signature Explorer
            is designed to help readers
            compare the composition of
            recorded fatal crashes
            across multiple dimensions.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- NEW CUSTOM D3 VISUALIZATION -->
      <article class="chart-card story-signature-card">

        <div class="chart-title-row">

          <h3 id="signature-chart-title">
            Fatality Signature Explorer
          </h3>

          <div class="signature-controls">

            <label for="signature-state">
              Select State
            </label>

            <select
              id="signature-state"
              aria-label="Choose a state for the signature">

              <option value="">
                United States
              </option>

            </select>

          </div>

        </div>

        <p>
          Investigate how temporal,
          environmental, and
          fatality-related measures
          combine into a state-level
          visual profile.
        </p>

        <!--
          New visualization mount point.

          A future js/fatalitySignature.js
          module will render the custom
          D3 visualization here.

          The existing js/main.js does
          not yet implement this view.
        -->

        <div
          id="fatality-signature"
          class="chart-content"
          aria-labelledby="signature-chart-title">

          <p class="chart-placeholder"
             role="status">
            Custom Fatality Signature
            visualization pending
            D3 implementation.
          </p>

        </div>

        <p class="story-method-note">
          This planned custom visualization
          will combine multiple data
          attributes using original
          visual encodings and interactive
          state comparisons.
        </p>

      </article>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          Why Combine Multiple Dimensions?
        </strong>

        <p>
          Separate charts help readers
          examine individual variables.
          A custom multidimensional
          visualization can support
          comparisons of several
          attributes within one
          consistent representation.
        </p>

        <p>
          The final innovative design
          must use custom D3 marks
          and encodings rather than
          simply linking conventional
          charts.
        </p>

      </aside>

      <p class="story-next">
        The guided story provides
        a sequence of questions.
        You can also investigate
        the underlying records
        yourself.

        <a href="#explorer">
          Continue to the interactive
          explorer →
        </a>
      </p>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         OPTIONAL EXPLORATION MODE
         EXISTING LINKED MAP + HEATMAP
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="explorer"
      class="chart-section"
      aria-labelledby="explorer-title">

      <div class="section-heading">

        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">
            OPTIONAL / EXPLORE THE DATA
          </p>

          <h2 id="explorer-title">
            Space-Time Crash Explorer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The guided story is complete,
            but the data can be explored
            in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the existing coordinated
            map and temporal heatmap
            to investigate recorded
            crash locations across
            selected states, weekdays,
            and hours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <button
          id="reset-filters"
          type="button"
          aria-label="Reset all dashboard filters">

          Reset All Filters

        </button>

      </div>

      <!-- EXISTING STATE CONTROL -->
      <div class="chart-title-row">

        <label for="explorer-state">
          Filter crash locations by state
        </label>

        <select
          id="explorer-state"
          aria-label="Select explorer state">

          <option value="">
            All States
          </option>

        </select>

      </div>

      <!-- EXISTING FEEDBACK -->
      <p
        id="explorer-feedback"
        class="explorer-feedback"
        aria-live="polite">

        Select a state or weekday-hour
        combination to explore fatal
        crash patterns.

      </p>

      <div class="chart-grid explorer-grid">

        <!-- EXISTING GEOGRAPHIC MAP -->
        <article class="chart-card">

          <h3 id="explorer-map-title">
            Geographic Crash Locations
          </h3>

          <p>
            Crash points represent
            recorded fatal crash
            locations.
          </p>

          <div
            id="explorer-map"
            class="chart-content"
            aria-labelledby="explorer-map-title">

            <p class="chart-placeholder"
               role="status">
              Loading geographic crash map...
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

        <!-- EXISTING LINKED HEATMAP -->
        <article class="chart-card">

          <h3 id="explorer-heatmap-title">
            Select Weekday and Hour
          </h3>

          <p>
            Select a time cell to
            highlight matching
            crash locations.
          </p>

          <div
            id="explorer-heatmap"
            class="chart-content"
            aria-labelledby="explorer-heatmap-title">

            <p class="chart-placeholder"
               role="status">
              Loading coordinated heatmap...
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

      </div>

      <aside class="story-insight">

        <strong>
          Explore With Context
        </strong>

        <p>
          Filtering reveals where
          recorded crashes occurred
          during a selected time.
          It does not establish
          which location or time
          is inherently more dangerous.
        </p>

      </aside>

    </section>

    <!-- ======================================
         STORY CONCLUSION
         ====================================== -->

    <section
      id="conclusion"
      class="story-conclusion"
      aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">

      <p class="eyebrow">
        THE TAKEAWAY
      </p>

      <h2 id="conclusion-title">
        A National Total Is
        Only the Beginning
      </h2>

      <p>
        Fatal Roads began with one
        national statistic and asked
        what that number could not
        explain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Geography revealed differences
        in recorded crash totals.
        Temporal visualizations showed
        how those records could be
        examined across months,
        weekdays, and hours.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recorded conditions provided
        additional context, while
        state-level comparisons
        distinguished crash totals
        from the number of fatalities.
      </p>

      <p>
        Together, these perspectives
        demonstrate why a national
        total is not enough to
        describe the distribution
        of fatal crashes.
      </p>

      <h3>
        What Can We Conclude?
      </h3>

      <p>
        The data supports descriptive
        comparisons across location,
        time, and recorded conditions.
        It does not, by itself,
        establish causal relationships
        or determine individual
        driving risk.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding these limitations
        is essential to interpreting
        fatal crash statistics responsibly.
      </p>

      <div class="story-actions">

        <a href="#overview"
           class="story-button">
          Return to Beginning ↑
        </a>

        <a href="#explorer"
           class="story-text-link">
          Explore the Data Again
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>

